.TH WREX-ESP 7 "2026-10-18" "wrex 0.1.0" "wrex Manual"
.SH NAME
wrex-esp \- esp layer for \fBwrex\fR(1)

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwrex\fR \fIcraft\fR|\fIsend\fR [options] \fB--layer\fR \fIesp\fR \fB--esp\-<field>\fR=\fIVALUE\fR ...
\fBwrex\fR \fIfields\fR \fIesp\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
IP encapsulating security payload (RFC 4303, next\-header 50): the 8\-byte fixed header — spi + seq — then everything after is opaque ciphertext (IV, payload, padding, pad length, next header). Craftable as the header + caller\-supplied payload; TERMINAL both ways: the encrypted payload stays payload, and nothing stacks on esp (the payload's next\-header byte is inside the ciphertext). NO decryption or integrity check — the standing crypto rule.
.

.SH ENCAPSULATION
Rank 25; stacks on ipv6 (parents).

.SH "FIELD FLAGS"
Dots in field paths become dashes in flags (ipv4.ttl -> --ipv4-ttl, --esp-spi).
Explicit values always win over autofill and are kept
unvalidated; \fB\-\-strict\fR re-validates (violations exit 2).
.TP
\fB\-\-esp\-spi=\fIINT\fR\fR
security parameters index Type u32. Default: 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-esp\-seq=\fIINT\fR\fR
sequence number Type u32. Default: 0.

.SH EXAMPLES
wrex fields esp

